# tests/gh-app-ratchet-by-design/ — the registry of scripts in bin/ that call
# `gh` raw ON PURPOSE and always will. ONE FILE PER SCRIPT, named for the
# script's path under bin/ with .txt appended, holding who decided it and a
# one-line summary; the operational reason lives on that script's own
# `# gh-identity: raw-by-design — <why>` line, so there is one copy of it.
#
# WHY THIS DIRECTORY EXISTS AT ALL (BH-1177 review). A permanent exception is a
# REVIEWED DECISION, not a sentence a script writes about itself. The shape this
# replaced — tests/gh-app-ratchet-allowlist.txt with `ALLOW_COUNT -eq 6` pinned
# next to it — said so out loud: "the count stays pinned so the list still grows
# only by a reviewed decision". Markers alone would have dropped that: a new
# script could ship one comment line, raise no finding, and quietly move the
# summary from 6 raw-by-design to 7. It was demonstrated on this very PR before
# this directory existed. So the pin comes back as an artifact instead of a
# number — one file, in the diff, where a reviewer reads it.
#
# It is the twin of tests/gh-app-ratchet-baseline/, and the two are mutually
# exclusive: a script is either QUEUED for conversion (baseline) or exempt from
# it (here), never both. tests/gh-app-ratchet-test.sh fails either way round.
#
# AN ENTRY IS A .txt FILE, and nothing else in here is — that is why this file
# has no extension. An entry that does not name a real script in bin/ carrying a
# `raw-by-design` marker is reported.
